        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked and Tested
          </h2>

          <p>
            Painter's tape looks like a commodity, and in one sense it is: the major brands all use crepe paper or thin poly backings with a pressure-sensitive adhesive, and a few pennies of difference per foot does not matter on a single room. What does matter is the adhesion level, the edge treatment and the removal window, and those three numbers vary far more than the colour of the roll suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            We started with every tape the major manufacturers publish specifications for, then cut the list to tapes with broad retail and online availability and a clear rating for surface, adhesion and removal days. Tapes that did not state a removal window, or stated one we could not confirm from the manufacturer, were dropped. So were novelty tapes sold mainly for crafts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Each tape went on the same set of test panels: smooth drywall with a cured eggshell finish, lightly textured drywall, a wall painted 24 hours earlier, primed pine trim, and window glass. We burnished each strip the same way, rolled a contrasting latex colour over the edge, and pulled the tape at a 45-degree angle once the paint was dry to the touch. We then looked for bleed under the edge, lifted base paint, and residue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Removal windows came from the manufacturers rather than from our own long-duration testing. We did leave several tapes in place for a week on a sunny window wall to see which ones started to bond or tear, but a single week in one house is not a substitute for the rated figures, and we say so where it matters. Ratings and review counts are rounded from what we saw on Amazon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="adhesion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Adhesion Levels, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every painter's tape is a compromise between two jobs that pull in opposite directions. It has to grip hard enough to seal the edge and stay put through two coats, and it has to let go cleanly without taking paint, finish or wallpaper with it. Manufacturers resolve that compromise differently for different surfaces, and that is what the adhesion level on the label is telling you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Medium adhesion is the default blue and green tape. It is designed for surfaces that are hard, cured and not easily damaged: trim with old paint, glass, tile, metal, and walls whose paint has been on for weeks. It holds through humidity and brushing, and it is the right choice for most masking. Low adhesion, usually sold in yellow or purple, is designed for surfaces that would be damaged by a stronger bond: paint that went on yesterday, wallpaper, cabinets with a factory finish, and finished wood floors.
          </p>

